  ('zcrm_1579374000144074067', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000108605002', 'Kirk Schults', NULL, '8-1-24: My reply:
Kirk,

This is a great question. Here''s the secret and I should have pointed this out in my earlier reply to you. It''s all in how the doctor "codes" the visit or procedure in the electronic medical record. 

If coded as "preventive" and it''s a procedure this is mandated to be part of Minimum Essential Coverage (MEC) then it would typically be a covered item. 

The moment the doctor codes it as "diagnostic", MEC providers like Planstin will deem that to be outside of preventive care and not pay it. 

This requires oversight on your part relative to what the doctor is doing during the visit. It''s a whole new world and the average doctor is not aware of these details and rely on people in the back office of the practice who manage insurance billing to handle it. 

Contact our concierge dept. for more. 

Best,

Kirk', 'Kirk Bennett', 'Kirk Bennett', '2024-08-01 10:59:32', '2024-08-01 10:59:32'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000144074062', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000108605002', 'Kirk Schults', NULL, '8-1-234: From Kirk Schults:

HI Kirk, aren’t some labs just routine and for preventative, like A1C , bloodwork etc. I get an annual physical every year and doc always orders bloodwork. I may ask for something like a B-6 test but that’s about it

 If I get a bill for say 2000, could I submit that ? I am only aware of 3 tests that were diagnostic, the others were routine I assumed.  How are we supposed to know what is routine or diagnostic? The doctor doesn’t know either.', 'Kirk Bennett', 'Kirk Bennett', '2024-08-01 10:53:01', '2024-08-01 10:53:01'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000144077018', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000131710002', 'Scott Beaton', NULL, '08.01.2024 Scott called to learn why the dermatology center won''t take Planstin as coverage, and if I can help him find a list of Dermatologists in the network.
I educated him on how his plan works regarding preventive, cash-pay patients, and sent him an email on HCBB and Healthgrades via Outlook. NT', 'Nahla Tebcherany', 'Nahla Tebcherany', '2024-08-01 10:44:24', '2024-08-01 10:44:24'),
  ('zcrm_1579374000144074057', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000108605002', 'Kirk Schults', NULL, '8-1-2024: What I replied with to Kirk:
Good Morning Kirk,

The Planstin coverage would pay for annual check-ups and the like. The moment the doctor finds something that is suspect (an issue/illness etc.) and orders labs or x-rays, this is diagnostic and is above and beyond the preventive care check-up; it  would not be paid for by Planstin. On items like that you would be out of pocket and the issue identified by the physician would qualify as a need. 

The preventive care add-on (Planstin in your case) keeps you qualified to continue contributing to your health savings account which should be used for anything that is out of pocket because you''re using pre-tax dollars.

Re the labs, it''s quite possible our concierge team can provide guidance on a low-cost lab in your area.  Contact them at 800-519-2969 M-F, 9 to 8 eastern. The concierge can also assist with identifying and clearing up Planstin questions.

I hope this helps.

Best,

Kirk', 'Kirk Bennett', 'Kirk Bennett', '2024-08-01 10:43:19', '2024-08-01 10:43:19'),
